hadiths[153]{hadithnumber,text}:
"22335","In the year two hundred and twenty-eight, Waki narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Abu Zabyan, from Muadh ibn Jabal, that when he returned from Yemen he said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw men in Yemen prostrating some of them to others, so shall we not prostrate to you? He said: If I were commanding a human to prostrate to a human, I would command the woman to prostrate to her husband."
"22336","Ibn Numayr narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Zabyan narrating from a man of the Ansar, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: Muadh came from Yemen and said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw men, then he mentioned its meaning."
"22337","Waki narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from Muadh, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Muadh, follow the evil deed with the good deed, it erases it, and mix with people with good character. Then he said, and Waki said: I found it in my book from Abu Dharr, and it is the first hearing. My father said, and Waki said: Sufyan said once, from Muadh."
"22338","Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Uthman, meaning ibn Mawhab, from Musa ibn Talhah, he said: With us is the writing of Muadh from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he only took charity from wheat and barley and raisins and dates."
"22339","Waki narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Jabir, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Muadh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me over Arab villages, and commanded me to take the share of the land."
"22340","And Abd al-Razzaq said, meaning from Sufyan, from Jabir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, meaning in the hadith of Muadh."
"22341","Waki narrated to us, from Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Muadh, he said: I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: O Muadh, do you know what the right of Allah upon the servants is? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: That you worship Him and associate nothing with Him. He said: So do you know what the right of the servants upon Allah is when they do that? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: That He does not punish them."
"22342","Waki narrated to us, from al-Nahhas ibn Qahm, Shaddad Abu Ammar narrated to me, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Six are from the signs of the Hour: my death, and the conquest of Bayt al-Maqdis, and a mortality that seizes among people like the plague of sheep, and a trial whose war enters the house of every Muslim, and that a man is given a thousand dinars yet is discontent with it, and that the Romans betray, so they march in eighty banners, under each banner twelve thousand."
"22343","Waki narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: We came to Muadh ibn Jabal and said: Narrate to us from the strange hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Yes, I was riding behind him upon a donkey, he said, so he said: O Muadh ibn Jabal. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you know what the right of Allah upon the servants is? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The right of Allah upon the servants is that they worship Him and associate nothing with Him. He said: Then he said: O Muadh. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you know what the right of the servants upon Allah is when they do that? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: That He does not punish them."
"22344","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, and Abd al-Razzaq said: Maar narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Do you know what the right of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, upon His servants is? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: That they worship Him and associate nothing with Him. He said: Do you know what the right of the servants upon Allah is when they do that? That He forgives them and does not punish them. Maar said in his hadith, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I not give people the glad tidings? He said: Leave them to work."
"22345","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, from Muadh, with its like."
"22346","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Razin, from Muadh, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not guide you to a door from the doors of Paradise? He said: And what is it? He said: There is no might nor power except with Allah."
"22347","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, Abu al-Tufayl narrated to us, Muadh ibn Jabal narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on a journey he traveled, and that was in the expedition of Tabuk, so he combined between Zuhr and Asr and Maghrib and Isha. I said: What carried him to that? He said: He wanted not to burden his nation."
"22348","Ismail narrated to us, Yunus narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Hissan ibn al-Kahin, he said: I entered the congregational mosque in Basra and sat to an old man white of head and beard, so he said: Muadh ibn Jabal narrated to me from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: There is no soul that dies while testifying that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah, that returning to a certain heart, except Allah forgives it. I said to him: Did you hear it from Muadh? So it was as if the people rebuked me. He said: Do not rebuke him nor blame him, leave him. Yes, I heard that from Muadh, narrating it precisely from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Ismail said once: Transmitting it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I said to some of them: Who is this? He said: This is Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah."
"22349","Abd al-Ala narrated to us, from Yunus, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Hissan ibn al-Kahin, he said, and his father was a soothsayer in the pre-Islamic age, he said: I entered the mosque in the rule of Uthman ibn Affan, and there was an old man white of head and beard narrating from Muadh, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the hadith."
"22350","Muhammad ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, meaning ibn Abi Uthman, Humayd ibn Hilal narrated to me, Hissan ibn al-Kahin al-Adawi narrated to us, he said: I sat in a sitting in which was Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, and I did not know him. He said: Muadh ibn Jabal narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no soul upon the earth that dies not associating anything with Allah, testifying that I am the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that returning to a certain heart, except it is forgiven. He said: I said: Did you hear this from Muadh ibn Jabal? He said: So the people rebuked me, so he said: Leave him, for he did not speak ill. Yes, I heard it from Muadh, claiming that he heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"22351","Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Habib ibn al-Shahid, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Hissan ibn al-Kahin, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, from Muadh, the like of it, near his saying."
"22352","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Yala ibn Ata ibn al-Walid ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Idris al-Abdi or al-Khawlani, he said: I sat in a sitting in which were twenty from the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and among them was a youth young in age, handsome of face, dark-eyed, bright-toothed, so when they differed about something and he said a saying, they ended at his saying, and behold it was Muadh ibn Jabal. So when it was the next day I came, and he was praying toward a pillar. He said: So he shortened his prayer, then sat wrapped in his cloak and fell silent. He said: So I said: By Allah, I love you for the majesty of Allah. He said: By Allah? He said: I said: By Allah. He said: For among those who love one another in Allah, in what I think he said, is in the shade of Allah on a day when there is no shade except His shade. Then there is no doubt in the rest of it, meaning in the rest of the hadith: Chairs of light are placed for them, the prophets and the truthful and the martyrs envy them for their sitting with the Lord, Mighty and Majestic. He said: So I narrated it to Ubadah ibn al-Samit, so he said: I will not narrate to you except what I heard from the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): My love is due for those who love one another in Me, and is due for those who exchange in Me, and My love is due for those who are truthful in Me and those who maintain ties. Shubah doubted about those who maintain ties or those who visit one another."
"22353","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from Muadh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies while testifying that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, truthfully from his heart, enters Paradise. Shubah said: I did not ask Qatadah whether he heard it from Anas."
"22354","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Husayn and al-Ashath ibn Sulaym, that they heard al-Aswad ibn Hilal narrating from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Muadh, do you know what the right of Allah upon the servants is? So he said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: They worship Him and associate nothing with Him. He said: Do you know what their right upon Him is when they do that? He said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: That He does not punish them."
"22355","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Hakim, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Abu al-Aswad al-Dili, he said: Muadh was in Yemen, so they referred to him about a Jew who died and left a Muslim brother, so Muadh said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Islam increases and does not decrease, so he gave him the inheritance."
"22356","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: I was the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Do you know what the right of Allah upon the servants is? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: That they worship Him and associate nothing with Him. He said: And do you know what their right upon Him is when they do that? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: That He does not punish them."
"22357","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Awn, from al-Harith ibn Amr, nephew of al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, from people of the companions of Muadh from the people of Hims, from Muadh, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he sent him to Yemen said: How will you act if a judgment is presented to you? He said: I judge by what is in the Book of Allah. He said: If it is not in the Book of Allah? He said: Then by the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: If it is not in the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I exert my opinion, sparing nothing. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck my chest, then said: Praise be to Allah who granted success to the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to what pleases the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"22358","Bahz narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, Qays ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Ramlah narrating from Abdullah ibn Muslim, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The possessor of three has made it binding. So Muadh said to him: And the possessor of two? He said: And the possessor of two."
"22359","Bahz narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas, that Muadh ibn Jabal narrated to him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Muadh ibn Jabal. He said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah, and blessedness to you. He said: No servant testifies that there is no god but Allah, then dies upon that, except he enters Paradise. He said: I said: Shall I not narrate to the people? He said: No, I fear that they will rely upon it."
"22360","Abu Kamil narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Tawus, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command me anything concerning the small numbers of cows."
"22361","Abu Kamil narrated to us, Hammad, meaning ibn Salamah, narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Muadh, then he mentioned its like."
"22362","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, and Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined between Zuhr and Asr and Maghrib and Isha in the expedition of Tabuk."
"22363","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Sufyan informed me, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Masruq, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to Yemen, so he commanded him to take from every thirty of cows a tabi or a tabiah, and from every forty a musinnah, and from every adult a dinar or its equivalent in Muafir garments."
"22364","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa narrated to us, Malik ibn Yukhamir narrated to us, that Muadh ibn Jabal narrated to them that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever fights in the path of Allah from a Muslim man for the pause of a she-camel's milking, Paradise is binding for him. And whoever asks Allah for death from himself truthfully, then dies or is killed, then he has the reward of a martyr. And whoever is wounded a wound in the path of Allah or suffers a blow, it comes on the Day of Resurrection at its fullest as it was, its color like saffron and its scent like musk. And whoever is wounded a wound in the path of Allah, upon him is the seal of the martyrs. My father said, and Hajjaj and Rawh said: Like the fullest, and Abd al-Razzaq said: Like the brightest, and this is the correct, if Allah wills."
"22365","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Maar narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal al-Adawi, from Abu Burdah, he said: Muadh ibn Jabal came to Abu Musa in Yemen, and there was a man with him. He said: What is this? He said: A man who was Jewish, then embraced Islam, then turned Jewish, and we have been urging him to Islam for, I think he said, two months. So he said: By Allah, I will not sit until you strike his neck. So his neck was struck. So he said: Allah and His Messenger decreed that whoever returns from his religion, kill him. Or he said: Whoever changes his religion, kill him."
"22366","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Maar informed us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Abu Wail, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, so one day I awoke near him while we were traveling, and I said: O Prophet of Allah, inform me of a deed that admits me to Paradise and distances me from the Fire. He said: You have asked about a great matter, and it is easy for whom Allah makes it easy: You worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and establish prayer, and give zakat, and fast Ramadan, and perform Hajj to the House. Then he said: Shall I not guide you to the doors of good? Fasting is a shield, and charity extinguishes sin, and the prayer of a man in the depth of the night. Then he recited His saying, Exalted: Their sides forsake their beds, until he reached: they do. Then he said: Shall I not inform you of the head of the matter and its pillar and the peak of its hump? So I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The head of the matter and its pillar is prayer, and the peak of its hump is jihad. Then he said: Shall I not inform you of the control of all that? So I said to him: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. So he took hold of his tongue and said: Restrain this upon you. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, are we indeed held accountable for what we speak? So he said: May your mother bereave you, O Muadh, and does anything topple people upon their faces in the Fire, or he said: upon their nostrils, except the harvests of their tongues?"
"22367","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Said al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Ward, meaning ibn Thumamah, and Yazid ibn Harun: The Jurayri informed me, from Abu al-Ward ibn Thumamah, both together, from al-Lajlaj, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man while he was saying: O Allah, I ask You for patience. So he said: You have asked for affliction, so ask Allah for well-being. He said: And he passed by a man saying: O Allah, I ask You for the completion of blessing. He said: O son of Adam, do you know what the completion of blessing is? He said: A supplication I supplicated hoping for good by it. He said: For the completion of blessing is escape from the Fire and entry into Paradise. My father said: If al-Jurayri had narrated only this hadith, it would have been."
"22368","Abd al-Razzaq and ibn Bakr narrated to us, they said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Tawus informed him that Muadh ibn Jabal said: I will not take anything concerning the small numbers of cows until I come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command me anything concerning them. Ibn Bakr said: I will not take concerning the small numbers."
"22369","Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus: Muadh was brought the small numbers of cows and honey, so he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command me anything concerning them. Sufyan said: The small numbers are what is less than thirty."
"22370","Al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, al-Awzai narrated to us, from Hassan ibn Atiyyah, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit narrated to me, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, he said: Muadh ibn Jabal came to us in Yemen as the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from before dawn, raising his voice with takbir, hoarse-voiced, so love for him was cast upon me, so I did not part from him until I threw dust upon him in Sham dead, may Allah have mercy upon him. Then I looked to the most knowledgeable of people after him, so I came to Abdullah ibn Masud, so he said to me: How will you be when rulers come upon you who pray prayer out of its time? He said: So I said: What do you command me if I reach that? He said: Pray the prayer at its time, and make that with them as supererogatory."
"22371","Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Amir al-Aslami narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Seek refuge with Allah from greed that leads to natural disposition, and from greed that leads to no object of hope, and from greed where there is no greed."
"22372","Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Muadh ibn Jabal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Their sides forsake their beds. He said: The standing of the servant at night."
"22373","Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Makhul, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The flourishing of Bayt al-Maqdis is the ruin of Yathrib, and the ruin of Yathrib is the emergence of the fierce battle, and the emergence of the fierce battle is the conquest of Constantinople, and the conquest of Constantinople is the emergence of the Dajjal. Then he struck upon his thigh, or upon his shoulder. Then he said: This is truth, as you are sitting. And Makhul used to narrate it from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Malik ibn Yukhamir, from Muadh ibn Jabal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), its like."
"22374","Yunus narrated to us, in the exegesis of Shayban, from Qatadah, he said: And Shahr ibn Hawshab narrated, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believers will be raised on the Day of Resurrection hairless, beardless, kohled, sons of thirty years."
"22375","Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash informed me, from Asim, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Malik al-Hudhali, from Muadh ibn Jabal and from Abu Musa, they said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) camped at a camp, those nearest to him were the Emigrants. He said: So we camped at a camp, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and we were around him. He said: So I and Muadh woke in the night and looked. He said: So we went out seeking him, when we heard a buzzing like the buzzing of millstones, when he came. So when he came he looked, he said: What is your affair? They said: We awoke and did not see you where you were, we feared something had befallen you, we came seeking you. He said: A comer came to me in my dream and gave me choice between that half my nation enters Paradise or intercession, so I chose intercession for them. So we said: We ask you by the right of Islam and by the right of companionship to admit us to Paradise. He said: So the people gathered upon him and said to him the like of our saying, and the people increased. So he said: I make my intercession for whoever dies not associating anything with Allah."
"22376","Rawh narrated to us, Hammad, meaning ibn Salamah, narrated to us, Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to us, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was guarded by his companions, then he mentioned its like."
"22377","Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, Abu Bakr, meaning ibn Ayyash, informed us, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: A man from the Ansar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I saw in sleep as if awake, I see a man descended from heaven wearing two green cloaks, descended upon the trunk of a wall in Madinah, so he called the adhan twice twice, then sat, then stood for prayer and said twice twice. He said: What good you saw, teach it to Bilal. He said: Umar said: I saw the like of that, but he preceded me."
"22378","Rawh narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever meets Allah not associating anything with Him, praying the five, and fasting Ramadan, he is forgiven. I said: Shall I not give them glad tidings, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Leave them to work."
"22379","Rawh narrated to us, Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, al-Ala ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Muadh ibn Jabal, that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Satan is the wolf of man, like the wolf of sheep, he takes the far and stray sheep, so beware of the ravines, and upon you is the congregation and the generality and the mosque."
"22380","Rawh narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, and Ishaq, meaning ibn Isa: Malik informed me, from Abu Hazim ibn Dinar, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, he said: I entered the mosque of Damascus in Sham, and I was with a youth bright of teeth, and the people were around him, when they differed about something they referred it to him and issued from his opinion. So I asked about him, so it was said: This is Muadh ibn Jabal. So when it was the next day I went early, and I found he had preceded me in early coming, and Ishaq said: In going at noon, and I found him praying, so I waited for him until when he finished his prayer I came to him from before his face and greeted him and said to him: By Allah, I love you for Allah, Mighty and Majestic. So he said: By Allah? So I said: By Allah. So he said: By Allah? So I said: By Allah. So he took hold of the fold of my cloak and pulled me to him and said: Rejoice, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: My love is due for those who love one another in Me, and those who sit together in Me, and those who visit one another in Me, and those who exchange in Me."
"22381","Rawh narrated to us, al-Hajjaj ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Muadh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Those who love one another in Allah are in the shade of the Throne on the Day of Resurrection."
"22382","Rawh narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Urwah ibn al-Nazzal or al-Nazzal ibn Urwah narrating from Muadh ibn Jabal, Shubah said: So I said to him: Did he hear it from Muadh? He said: He did not hear it from him, though he met him, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me of a deed that admits me to Paradise, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Maar from Asim, that al-Hakam said: And I heard it from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib."
"22383","Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz, meaning ibn Muslim, narrated to us, al-Husayn narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Muadh, he said: The people at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when a man was preceded by part of his prayer, would ask them, so they would gesture to him about what he was preceded by of prayer, so he would begin and make up what he was preceded by, then enter with the people in their prayer. So Muadh ibn Jabal came while the people were sitting in their prayer, so he sat. So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he stood and made up what he had been preceded by. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do as Muadh did."
"22384","Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid, meaning ibn Jafar, informed me, Salih, meaning ibn Abi Arib, narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: Muadh said to us in his illness: I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something I was concealing from you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever his last speech is: There is no god but Allah, Paradise is binding for him."
"22385","Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Amash narrating from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Musab ibn Sad, that Muadh said: By Allah, Umar is in Paradise, and I would not love that I have red camels and that you parted before I inform you why I said that. Then he narrated to them the dream which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw concerning Umar. He said: And the dream of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is truth."
"22386","Hammad ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk would not depart until it cooled, until he combined between Zuhr and Asr and Maghrib and Isha."
"22387","Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Hashimi narrated to us, Abu Bakr, meaning ibn Ayyash, narrated to us, Asim narrated to us, from Abu Wail, from Muadh, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen, and commanded me to take from every adult a dinar or its equivalent in Muafir, and commanded me to take from every forty cows a musinnah, and from every thirty cows a yearling tabi, and commanded me concerning what the sky waters, the tenth, and what is watered by buckets, half the tenth."
"22388","Muhammad ibn Musab narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Jabir, from a man, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever equips a fighter or succeeds him in his family with good, he is with us."
"22389","Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said to me: O Muadh, do you know what the right of Allah upon the servants is? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: They worship Him and associate nothing with Him. Do you know what the right of the servants upon Allah is when they do that? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: He admits them to Paradise."
"22390","Affan and Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, Hasan said in his hadith: Ali ibn Zayd informed me, from Abu al-Malih, al-Hasan said: al-Hudhali, from Rawh ibn Abid, from Abu al-Awwam, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon a red camel, so he said: O Muadh. I said: At your service. He said: Do you know what the right of Allah upon the servants is? He said: So I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said it three times, and I said that three times. Then he said: His right, Mighty and Majestic, is that they worship Him and associate nothing with Him. Then he said: Do you know what the right of the servants upon Allah is when they do that? So I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said it three times, and I said that three times. So he said: Their right upon Him when they do that is that He forgives them and admits them to Paradise."
"22391","Affan and Husn narrated to us, they said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Razin, from Muadh ibn Jabal, its like, except that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a donkey upon which a packsaddle had been fastened, except that Hasan joined the two isnads in his hadith."
"22392","Haywah ibn Shurayh and Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, they said: Baqiyyah, and he is ibn al-Walid, narrated to us, Bahir ibn Sad narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Abu Bahriyyah, from Muadh ibn Jabal, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Fighting is two fightings. As for whoever seeks the face of Allah and obeys the imam and spends the precious and eases the partner and avoids corruption, his sleep and his wakefulness are all reward. And as for whoever fights for pride and showing off and repute and disobeys the imam and corrupts in the earth, he does not return with sufficiency."
"22393","Haywah ibn Shurayh and Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, they said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Bahir ibn Sad narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Abu Bahriyyah, from Muadh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Laylat al-Qadr, so he said: It is in the last ten, or in the fifth, or in the third."
"22394","Al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, Abdullah said, and al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, ibn Ayyash narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Muadh, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Caution will never benefit against decree, but supplication benefits from what descended and from what did not descend, so upon you is supplication, servants of Allah."
"22395","Abu al-Mughirah and Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, they said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Sufyan ibn Abi Maryam narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Qutayb al-Sakuni, from Abu Bahriyyah, Abu al-Mughirah said in his hadith: From Abdullah ibn Qays, he said: I heard Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The great fierce battle and the conquest of Constantinople and the emergence of the Dajjal are in seven months."
"22396","Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, Abu Bakr narrated to us, Damrah ibn Habib narrated to us, from a man, from Muadh ibn Jabal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When circumcision passes circumcision, ghusl is binding."
"22397","Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, Abu Bakr narrated to us, Atiyyah ibn Qays narrated to me, from Muadh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jihad is the pillar of Islam and the peak of its hump."
"22398","Rawh and Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Zabyah, from Muadh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim who sleeps upon the remembrance of Allah pure, then wakes in the night and asks Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for good from the affair of this world and the Hereafter, except He gives it to him. Hasan said in his hadith, he said: Thabit al-Bunani came to us here and narrated this hadith from Muadh. Abu Salamah said: I think he means Abu Zabyah."
"22399","Rawh narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit, he said: Abu Zabyah came to us and narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of this hadith."
"22400","Al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated to us, ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Bahir ibn Sad, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Malik ibn Yukhamir, from Muadh ibn Jabal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever fights in the path of Allah for the pause of a she-camel, Paradise is binding for him. And the pause of a she-camel is as much as its milk flows for one who milks it."
"22401","Al-Hakam ibn Nafi' narrated to us, Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The peak of the hump of Islam is jihad in the cause of Allah."
"22402","Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, Safwan narrated to us, Rashid ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Asim ibn Humayd, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to Yemen, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with him advising him, while Mu'adh was riding and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was walking beneath his mount. When he finished he said: O Mu'adh, perhaps you will not meet me after this year of mine, or perhaps you will pass by this mosque of mine or my grave. So Mu'adh wept out of grief at parting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he turned and faced toward Madinah and said: Indeed the most entitled of people to me are the God-fearing, whoever they are and wherever they are."
"22403","Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, Safwan narrated to us, Abu Ziyad Yahya ibn Ubayd al-Ghassani narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Qutayb, from Mu'adh, that he used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen and said: Perhaps you will pass by my grave and my mosque. I have sent you to a people whose hearts are tender; they fight for the truth twice. So fight, with those of them who obey you, those who disobey you. Then they will return to Islam until the woman hastens ahead of her husband, and the child his father, and the brother his brother. So settle between the two tribes, al-Sakun and al-Sakasik."
"22404","Al-Hakam ibn Nafi' Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Safwan ibn Amr narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Asim ibn Humayd al-Sakuni, that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Mu'adh to Yemen, he went out to Yemen with him, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advising him, while Mu'adh was riding and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was walking beneath his mount. When he finished he said: O Mu'adh, perhaps you will not meet me after this year of mine, and perhaps you will pass by my mosque and my grave. So Mu'adh ibn Jabal wept out of grief at parting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not weep, O Mu'adh; weeping - or: indeed weeping - is from Satan."
"22405","Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Maryam al-Ghassani narrated to us, from Habib ibn Ubayd, from Mu'adh, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In the end of time there will be peoples, brothers in public but enemies in secret. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, how can that be? He said: That will be because some of them desire what is with others, and some of them fear others."
"22406","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu al-Ward, from al-Lajlaj, Mu'adh narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon a man while he was praying, and he was saying in his supplication: O Allah, I ask You for patience. He said: You have asked for affliction, so ask Allah for well-being. He said: And he came upon a man while he was saying: O Allah, I ask You for the completion of Your favor. So he said: O son of Adam, do you know what completion of favor is? He said: O Messenger of Allah, a supplication I made hoping for good through it. He said: The completion of favor is salvation from the Fire and entry into Paradise. And he came upon a man while he was saying: O Possessor of Majesty and Honor. So he said: It has been answered for you, so ask."
"22407","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Shu'bah, Amr ibn Abi Hakim narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar, from Abu al-Aswad, he said: A Jew whose heir was a Muslim was brought to Mu'adh, so he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say - or he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam increases and does not decrease. So he gave him the inheritance."
"22408","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Mu'awiyah, and he is al-Darir, narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu Sufyan, from Anas, he said: We came to Mu'adh and said: Narrate to us from the unusual hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a donkey, and he said: O Mu'adh. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you know what the right of Allah upon the servants is? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The right of Allah upon the servants is that they worship Him and not associate anything with Him. Do you know what the right of the servants upon Allah is if they do that? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The right of the servants upon Allah if they do that is that He not punish them."
"22409","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ismail narrated to us, from Layth, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from Mu'adh, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, advise me. He said: Fear Allah wherever you are. He said: Give me more. He said: Follow up the evil deed with a good deed; it will erase it. He said: Give me more. He said: Deal with people with good character."
"22410","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr, meaning Ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: I am one who witnessed Mu'adh when death came to him, saying: Uncover from me the curtain of the tent; I will narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he said once: I will inform you of something I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); nothing prevented me from narrating it to you except that you might rely upon it. I heard him say: Whoever testifies that there is no god but Allah, sincerely from his heart, or with certainty from his heart, the Fire will not enter him - or: he will enter Paradise - and he said once: He will enter Paradise and the Fire will not touch him."
"22411","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Waki' narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Awn al-Thaqafi, from al-Harith ibn Amr, from men among the companions of Mu'adh, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sent him to Yemen, said: How will you judge? He said: I will judge by the Book of Allah. He said: If it is not in the Book of Allah? He said: Then by the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: If it is not in the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I will exert my own judgment. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Praise be to Allah who has granted success to the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"22412","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Mu'adh, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha, in the expedition of Tabuk."
"22413","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Waki' narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahram narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Mu'adh, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: May your mother be bereft of you; and does anything throw people upon their nostrils in Hell except the harvests of their tongues."
"22414","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Waki' narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Burqan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Marzuq, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Abu Muslim al-Khawlani, he said: I came to the mosque of the people of Damascus, and there was a circle in it with elderly men among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a youth among them with dark-lined eyes and shining teeth; whenever they differed about something they referred it to the young man, a youthful lad. I said to a companion of mine: Who is this? He said: This is Mu'adh ibn Jabal. So I came in the evening and they did not attend. So I came early in the morning and they did not come. Then I went in the afternoon and there was the youth praying toward a pillar. So I bowed, then I turned to him. He gave salam, and I drew near to him and said: I love you for Allah. So he pulled me toward him and said: What did you say? I said: I love you for Allah. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) relating from his Lord, saying: Those who love one another for Allah are upon pulpits of light in the shade of the Throne on a day when there is no shade except His shade. So I went out until I met Ubadah ibn al-Samit and mentioned to him the hadith of Mu'adh ibn Jabal. So he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) relating from his Lord, mighty and majestic, saying: My love is due for those who love one another in Me, and My love is due for those who give to one another in Me, and My love is due for those who visit one another in Me. And those who love one another for Allah are upon pulpits of light in the shade of the Throne on a day when there is no shade except His shade."
"22415","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Abbas narrated to us, Abu al-Malih narrated to us, Habib ibn Abi Marzuq narrated to us, from Ata, Abu Muslim narrated to us, he said: I entered the mosque of Hims, and there was a circle in it with thirty-two men among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and among them a dark-eyed youth. Then he mentioned the hadith."
"22416","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hariz, meaning Ibn Uthman, informed us, Rashid ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Asim ibn Humayd al-Sakuni, and he was among the companions of Mu'adh ibn Jabal, from Mu'adh, he said: We watched for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the Isha prayer, and he was delayed until we thought he would not come out, and some of us were saying: He has prayed and will not come out. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out, and we said: O Messenger of Allah, we thought you would not come out, and some of us were saying: He has prayed and will not come out. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Delay this prayer, for you have been favored by it over the rest of the nations, and no nation before you prayed it."
"22417","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hashim, meaning Ibn al-Qasim, narrated to us, Hariz narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Asim ibn Humayd al-Sakuni, and he was among the companions of Mu'adh: I heard Mu'adh say: We watched for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meaning we waited for him. Then he mentioned its meaning."
"22418","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Urwah ibn al-Nazzal narrating from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the expedition of Tabuk. When I saw him alone, I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me of a deed that will admit me into Paradise. He said: Bravo; you have asked about a great matter, and it is easy for the one for whom Allah makes it easy: You establish the prescribed prayer, and you give the obligatory zakat, and you meet Allah, mighty and majestic, not associating anything with Him. Shall I not direct you to the head of the matter, and its pillar, and the peak of its hump? As for the head of the matter, it is Islam; whoever submits is safe. As for its pillar, it is prayer. As for the peak of its hump, it is jihad in the cause of Allah. Shall I not direct you to the doors of good? Fasting is a shield, and charity, and the servant standing in the depths of the night expiate sins. And he recited this verse: Their sides forsake their beds, calling upon their Lord in fear and hope, and from what We have provided them they spend. Shall I not direct you to what controls all of that for you? Some people came, and I feared they would distract the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from me - Shu'bah said, or a word like it - so I said: O Messenger of Allah, your saying: Shall I not direct you to what controls all of that for you? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pointed with his hand to his tongue. I said: O Messenger of Allah, will we be taken to account for what we speak? He said: May your mother be bereft of you, Mu'adh; does anything throw people upon their nostrils except the harvests of their tongues. Shu'bah said: Al-Hakam said to me: Maymun ibn Abi Shabib narrated it to me, and al-Hakam said: I heard it from him forty years ago."
"22419","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Abu Ramlah, from Ubaydullah ibn Muslim, from Mu'adh, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The possessor of three has made it obligatory. So Mu'adh said: And the possessor of two, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And the possessor of two."
"22420","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, he said: I read to Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi: Malik narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, from Abu al-Tufayl Amir ibn Wathilah, that Mu'adh informed him that they went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Tabuk. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to combine Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha. He said: He delayed the prayer, then went out and prayed Zuhr and Asr together, then entered, then went out and prayed Maghrib and Isha together. Then he said: Tomorrow, if Allah wills, you will come to the spring of Tabuk, and you will not come to it until the day is bright. Whoever comes to it, let him not touch any of its water until I come. So we came, and two men had preceded us to it, and the spring was like a shoelace, oozing a little water. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked them: Did you touch any of its water? They said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reviled them and said to them what Allah willed him to say. Then they scooped with their hands from the spring, little by little, until it gathered in something. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) washed in it his face and his hands, then returned it into it. So the spring flowed with abundant water, and the people drew water. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Soon, O Mu'adh, if your life is prolonged, you will see water here that has filled gardens."
"22421","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Rawh narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, that Abu al-Tufayl informed him that Mu'adh ibn Jabal informed him. Then he mentioned its meaning, and said: Oozing a little water."
"22422","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ali ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Abdullah informed us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, that Ubaydullah ibn Zahr narrated to him, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from Abu Ayyash, he said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you wish, I will inform you of what is the first thing Allah, mighty and majestic, says to the believers on the Day of Resurrection, and what is the first thing they say to Him. We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Allah, mighty and majestic, says to the believers: Did you love meeting Me? They say: Yes, our Lord. He says: Why? They say: We hoped for Your pardon and Your forgiveness. So He says: My forgiveness has become obligatory for you."
"22423","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Shu'ayb informed us, Abdullah ibn Abi Husayn narrated to me, Shahr ibn Hawshab narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm - and he is the one whom Umar ibn al-Khattab sent to Sham to teach the people jurisprudence - that Mu'adh ibn Jabal narrated to him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that one day he rode upon a donkey of his called Ufayr, whose rein was of palm fiber. Then he said: Ride, O Mu'adh. I said: Go on, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Ride. So I rode behind him, and the donkey threw us off. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood up laughing, and I stood up reproaching myself with regret. Then he did that a second time, then a third time. Then he rode, and the donkey went with us. Then he put his hand behind him and struck my back with a whip he had, or a stick. Then he said: O Mu'adh, do you know what the right of Allah upon the servants is? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The right of Allah upon the servants is that they worship Him and not associate anything with Him. Then he went on as Allah willed. Then he put his hand behind him and struck my back and said: O Mu'adh, O son of the mother of Mu'adh, do you know what the right of the servants upon Allah is if they do that? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The right of the servants upon Allah if they do that is that He admit them into Paradise."
"22424","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to me, Dubarah ibn Abdullah narrated to me, from Duwayd ibn Nafi', from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Mu'adh, that Allah guide through your hands a man from the people of polytheism is better for you than red camels belonging to you."
"22425","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash informed us, from Safwan ibn Amr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Hadrami, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me ten words. He said: Do not associate anything with Allah, even if you are killed and burned. Do not disobey your parents, even if they command you to leave your family and your wealth. Do not deliberately abandon a prescribed prayer, for whoever deliberately abandons a prescribed prayer, the protection of Allah is absolved from him. Do not drink wine, for it is the head of every obscenity. Beware of disobedience, for by disobedience the wrath of Allah, mighty and majestic, descends. Beware of fleeing from the battlefield, even if the people perish. If death befalls the people while you are among them, remain firm. Spend upon your dependents from your means. Do not lift your stick from them for discipline, and make them fear Allah."
"22426","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from al-Walibi, a friend of Mu'adh ibn Jabal, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is put in charge of any of the affairs of the people, then veils himself from the weak and needy, Allah will veil Himself from him on the Day of Resurrection."
"22427","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, al-Bara' al-Ghanawi narrated to us, al-Hasan narrated to us, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited this verse: The companions of the right, and the companions of the left. Then he grasped with his two hands two handfuls and said: This is in Paradise, and I do not care; and this is in the Fire, and I do not care."
"22428","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hashim narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Shahr ibn Hawshab narrated to us, Aidhullah ibn Abdullah narrated to us, that Mu'adh came to Yemen, and a woman from Khawlan met him with twelve sons of hers; she had left their father in her house, the youngest of whom whose beard had grown full. She stood and greeted Mu'adh, while two of her sons held her upper arms. She said: Who sent you, O man? Mu'adh said to her: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me. The woman said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent you, and you are the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); will you not inform me, O messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Mu'adh said to her: Ask me about whatever you wish. She said: Narrate to me what is the right of the husband over his wife. Mu'adh said to her: She fears Allah as much as she can, and listens and obeys. She said: I adjure you by Allah to narrate to me what is the right of the man over his wife. Mu'adh said to her: Were you not content to listen and obey and fear Allah? She said: Yes, but narrate to me what is the right of the husband over his wife, for I left the father of these, an old shaykh, in the house. So Mu'adh said to her: By the One in whose hand is the soul of Mu'adh, if when you returned to him you found leprosy had eaten his flesh and pierced his nostrils, and you found his nostrils flowing with pus and blood, then you put your mouth to them in order to attain his right, you would never attain that, ever."
"22429","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hujayn ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz, meaning Ibn Abi Salamah, narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad, freedman of Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, that it reached him from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No son of Adam ever did a deed more saving for him from the punishment of Allah than the remembrance of Allah."
"22430","And Mu'adh said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you of the best of your deeds, and the purest of them with your King, and the highest of them in your ranks, and better for you than giving gold and silver, and than meeting your enemy tomorrow so you strike their necks and they strike your necks? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The remembrance of Allah, mighty and majestic."
"22431","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Kathir ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ja'far, meaning Ibn Burqan, narrated to us, Habib ibn Abi Marzuq narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Abu Muslim al-Khawlani, he said: I entered the mosque of Hims, and in it were about thirty elderly men among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Among them was a youth with dark-lined eyes and shining teeth, silent. When the people disputed about something, they turned to him and asked him. I said to a companion of mine: Who is this? He said: This is Mu'adh ibn Jabal. So love for him fell into my soul. I was with them until they dispersed. Then I came early to the mosque, and Mu'adh ibn Jabal was standing praying toward a pillar. He was silent and did not speak to me. So I prayed, then sat and gathered myself in a cloak of mine. Then he sat and was silent and did not speak to me, and I was silent and did not speak to him. Then I said: By Allah, I love you. He said: For what do you love me? I said: For Allah, blessed and exalted. So he took hold of my gathered cloak and pulled me toward him a little. Then he said: Rejoice, if you are truthful. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Those who love one another in My majesty will have pulpits of light envied by the prophets and the martyrs. So I went out and met Ubadah ibn al-Samit and said: O Abu al-Walid, shall I not narrate to you what Mu'adh ibn Jabal narrated to me about those who love one another? He said: I will narrate to you from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), raising it to the Lord, mighty and majestic. He said: My love is due for those who love one another in Me, and My love is due for those who visit one another in Me, and My love is due for those who give to one another in Me, and My love is due for those who keep ties with one another in Me."
"22432","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata al-Khaffaf al-Ijli narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Mu'adh, he said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believers will be resurrected on the Day of Resurrection hairless, beardless, with kohl, thirty years old."
"22433","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, Sulayman al-A'mash narrated to me, from Raja' al-Ansari, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking him, and it was said to me: He went out earlier. So whenever I passed anyone, he said: He passed earlier, until I passed and found him standing praying. So I came until I stood behind him. He prolonged the prayer. When he finished the prayer, I said: O Messenger of Allah, you prayed a long prayer. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I prayed a prayer of desire and fear. I asked Allah, mighty and majestic, for three things: He gave me two and withheld one from me. I asked Him not to destroy my nation by drowning, and He gave it to me. I asked Him not to make an enemy outside of them prevail over them, and He gave it to me. I asked Him not to make their violence among themselves, and He returned it to me."
"22434","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik, from Mu'adh, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Mu'adh, whoever dies not associating anything with Allah will enter Paradise."
"22435","Mu'awiyah ibn Amr and Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us - Harun said in his hadith, he said - and Haywah said, from Ibn Abi Habib - and Mu'awiyah said: from Haywah, from Yazid, from Salamah ibn Usamah, from Yahya ibn al-Hakam - that Mu'adh said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to collect the charities of the people of Yemen, and commanded me to take from cattle, from every thirty, a tabi' - Harun said: a tabi' is a jadha' or jadh'ah - and from every forty, a musinnah. He said: They offered me to take from the forties - Harun said: what is between forty or fifty, and between sixty and seventy, and what is between eighty and ninety - but I refused that and said to them: Until I ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that. So I came and informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he commanded me to take from every thirty a tabi', and from every forty a musinnah, and from sixty two tabi's, and from seventy a musinnah and a tabi', and from eighty two musinnahs, and from ninety three followers, and from one hundred a musinnah and two tabi's, and from one hundred and ten two musinnahs and a tabi', and from one hundred and twenty three musinnahs or four followers. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me not to take anything in between that - and Harun said: anything in between that - unless it reaches a musinnah or a jadha'. And he claimed that there is no obligation in the awqas."
"22436","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Said, freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, Thabit ibn Yazid narrated to us, Asim narrated to us, from Abu Munib al-Ahdab, he said: Mu'adh delivered a sermon in Sham and mentioned the plague. He said: It is the mercy of your Lord, and the supplication of your Prophet, and the taking of the righteous before you. O Allah, give the family of Mu'adh their share of this mercy. Then he descended from that station of his and entered upon Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'adh. Abd al-Rahman said: The truth is from your Lord, so do not be among the doubters. Mu'adh said: You will find me, if Allah wills, among the patient."
"22437","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Said narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, Abd al-Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Mu'adh, he said: Two men reviled one another before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and one of them became angry until it seemed to me that his nose would be torn apart from anger. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I know a word which, if this angry man said it, the anger would leave him: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the accursed Satan."
"22438","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Surayj ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz, meaning al-Darawardi, narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays the five prayers, and performs Hajj of the Sacred House, and fasts Ramadan - and I do not know whether he mentioned zakat or not - it is a right upon Allah to forgive him, whether he emigrates in His cause or remains in the land where he was born. So Mu'adh said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I inform the people? He said: Leave the people, O Mu'adh. In Paradise there are one hundred levels; between every two levels is one hundred years. Al-Firdaws is the highest of Paradise and its middle, and from it the rivers of Paradise burst forth. So when you ask Allah, ask Him for al-Firdaws."
"22439","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Masarrah ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Ubaydullah, he said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: You will emigrate to Sham, and it will be opened for you, and among you will be a disease like a boil or like a blister that seizes a man by the throat; Allah takes their souls through it as martyrdom and purifies their deeds thereby. O Allah, if You know that Mu'adh ibn Jabal heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then give him and his household the fullest share of it. So the plague struck them and none of them remained. Then he was struck in his index finger, and he used to say: Nothing would please me more in exchange for it than red camels."
"22440","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ahmad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Harrani narrated to us, Ubaydullah, meaning Ibn Amr, narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: Two men of the Children of Israel traced their lineage in the time of Musa, peace be upon him, one of them a Muslim and the other a polytheist. The polytheist traced his lineage and said: I am so-and-so son of so-and-so, until he reached nine forefathers. Then he said to his companion: Trace your lineage, may you have no mother. He said: I am so-and-so son of so-and-so, and I am free from what is beyond that. So Musa, peace be upon him, called the people and gathered them, then said: It has been judged between you two. As for the one who traced himself to nine forefathers, you are above them, the tenth, in the Fire. As for the one who traced himself to his two parents, you are a man from the people of Islam."
"22441","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Affan narrated to us, Khalid, meaning al-Tahhan, narrated to us, Yahya al-Taymi informed us, from Ubaydullah ibn Muslim, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There are no two Muslims who lose three children by death except that Allah admits them into Paradise by virtue of His mercy toward them. They said: O Messenger of Allah, or two? He said: Or two. They said: Or one? He said: Or one. Then he said: By the One in whose hand is my soul, the miscarried fetus will drag its mother by its umbilical cord to Paradise if she seeks reward for it."
"22442","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies not associating anything with Allah will enter Paradise. And Hammad said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said it to Mu'adh."
"22443","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Affan narrated to us, Hammad, meaning Ibn Salamah, narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Bahdalah and I and Thabit were together, and Asim narrated from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Zabyah, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim who sleeps upon the remembrance of Allah, in a state of purity, then wakes in the night and asks Allah for good from this world and the Hereafter, except that He gives it to him. So Thabit said: He came to us and narrated this hadith to us, and I do not know him except meaning Abu Zabyah. I said to Hammad: From Mu'adh? He said: From Mu'adh."
"22444","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Qutaybah ibn Said narrated to us, Ibn Luhay'ah narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Yazid, from Ali ibn Rabah, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entrusted us with five things: whoever does any of them is guaranteed by Allah: whoever visits a sick person, or goes out with a funeral, or goes out as a warrior in the cause of Allah, or enters upon a ruler intending thereby to honor and respect him, or sits in his house so the people are safe from him and he is safe."
"22445","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Qutaybah ibn Said narrated to us, Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Tufayl Amir ibn Wathilah, from Mu'adh, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk, when he departed before the sun declined, would delay Zuhr until he combined it with Asr, praying them together. When he departed after the sun declined, he prayed Zuhr and Asr together, then marched. When he departed before Maghrib, he delayed Maghrib until he prayed it with Isha. When he departed after Maghrib, he hastened Isha and prayed it with Maghrib."
"22446","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us - Abdullah said: And I heard it myself from Harun - Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi' al-Tanukhi, judge of Ifriqiyyah, that Mu'adh ibn Jabal came to Sham while the people of Sham did not pray witr. So he said to Mu'awiyah: Why do I see the people of Sham not praying witr? Mu'awiyah said: Is that obligatory upon them? He said: Yes. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: My Lord, mighty and majestic, has added for me a prayer, and it is witr; its time is between Isha and the rising of dawn."
"22447","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Affan narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas, that Mu'adh ibn Jabal narrated to him, he said: While I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with nothing between me and him except the back of the saddle, he said: O Mu'adh. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah, and may you be granted happiness. Then he traveled a while, then said: O Mu'adh ibn Jabal. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah, and may you be granted happiness. Then he traveled a while, then said: O Mu'adh ibn Jabal. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah, and may you be granted happiness. He said: Do you know what the right of Allah upon the servants is? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The right of Allah upon the servants is that they worship Him and not associate anything with Him. Then he traveled a while, then said: O Mu'adh ibn Jabal. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah, and may you be granted happiness. He said: Do you know what the right of the servants upon Allah is if they do that? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The right of the servants upon Allah is that He not punish them."
"22448","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas, from Mu'adh, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it or like it."
"22449","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Bahz narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mu'adh, he said: I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with nothing between me and him except the back of the saddle. Then he mentioned similar to it."
"22450","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Razin, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not direct you to a door among the doors of Paradise? I said: Yes. He said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah."
"22451","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Affan narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Abu Awn informed me, he said: I heard al-Harith ibn Amr, son of the brother of al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, narrating from people among the companions of Mu'adh, from the people of Hims, from Mu'adh, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Mu'adh ibn Jabal when he sent him to Yemen - then he mentioned: How will you judge if a case arises? He said: I will judge by the Book of Allah. He said: If it is not in the Book of Allah? He said: Then by the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: If it is not in the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I will exert my judgment and not fall short. So he struck my chest and said: Praise be to Allah who has granted success to the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what pleases His Messenger."
"22452","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Buhayr ibn Sa'd, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: No woman harms her husband in this world except that his wife among the houris says: Do not harm him; may Allah fight you, for he is only a guest with you, about to leave you for us."
"22453","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: The keys of Paradise are the testimony that there is no god but Allah."
"22454","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Their sides forsake their beds, calling upon their Lord in fear and hope - he said: The servant standing in prayer at night."
"22455","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Qutaybah ibn Said narrated to us, Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from Rabiah ibn Yazid, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Yazid ibn Umayrah, he said: When death came to Mu'adh ibn Jabal, it was said to him: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, advise us. He said: Seat me. Then he said: The place of knowledge and faith is: whoever seeks them finds them - he said it three times. So seek knowledge with four groups: with Uwaymir Abu al-Darda, and with Salman al-Farisi, and with Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and with Abdullah ibn Salam, who was a Jew then embraced Islam. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: He is the tenth of ten in Paradise."
"22456","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Surayj ibn al-Nu'man and Yunus narrated to us, they said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Sari ibn Yan'um, from Marih ibn Masruq, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sent him to Yemen, said: Beware of luxury, for the servants of Allah are not luxurious."
"22457","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, Imran narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - or: he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The people of Paradise will enter Paradise hairless, beardless, with kohl, thirty or thirty-three years old."
"22458","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated to us, Umar ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Ziyad, from a man he trusted who narrated to him, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Satan is the wolf of man, like the wolf of sheep; he takes the far-off and isolated sheep. Beware of the mountain paths, and hold to the congregation and the generality."
"22459","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Ibn Umayr, Abd al-Malik, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Mu'adh, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed a prayer and perfected the bowing, prostration, and standing in it. I mentioned that to him, and he said: This is a prayer of desire and fear. I asked my Lord in it for three things: He gave me two and did not give me one. I asked Him not to kill my nation with a year of hunger so they perish, and He gave me it. I asked Him not to empower an enemy outside of them over them, and He gave me it. I asked Him not to make their violence among themselves, and He withheld it from me."
"22460","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Said, freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, Jahdam, meaning al-Yamami, narrated to us, Yahya, meaning Ibn Abi Kathir, narrated to us, Zayd, meaning Ibn Abi Sallam, narrated to us, from Abu Sallam - and he is Zayd ibn Sallam ibn Abi Sallam, attributing him to his grandfather - that Abd al-Rahman ibn Aish al-Hadrami narrated to him, from Malik ibn Yukhamir, that Mu'adh ibn Jabal said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was held back from us one morning from the dawn prayer until we almost saw the edge of the sun. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out quickly, the call for prayer was made, and he prayed, shortening his prayer. When he gave salam, he said: Remain as you are in your rows. Then he turned to us and said: I will tell you what held me back from you this morning. I stood in the night and prayed what was decreed for me, then I dozed in my prayer until I woke, and there I was with my Lord, mighty and majestic, in the finest form. He said: O Muhammad, do you know concerning what the highest assembly disputes? I said: I do not know, my Lord. He said: O Muhammad, concerning what does the highest assembly dispute? I said: I do not know, my Lord. Then I saw Him place His palm between my shoulders until I felt the coolness of His fingertips between my chest. Then everything became manifest to me, and I knew. He said: O Muhammad, concerning what does the highest assembly dispute? I said: Concerning expiations. He said: What are the expiations? I said: Carrying the feet to the congregational prayers, and sitting in the mosques after prayer, and perfecting ablution in difficult circumstances. He said: What are the ranks? I said: Feeding food, and gentle speech, and prayer while people sleep. He said: Ask. I said: O Allah, I ask You for doing good deeds, and abandoning evil deeds, and love of the poor, and that You forgive me and have mercy on me, and when You intend a trial for a people, take me without being tried, and I ask You for Your love, and the love of whoever loves You, and the love of a deed that brings me near to Your love. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are true, so study them and learn them."
"22461","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Zayd ibn Yahya al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Ibn Thawban narrated to us, from his father, from Makhul, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from Malik ibn Yukhamir al-Saksaki, he said: I heard Mu'adh say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is wounded with a wound in the cause of Allah will come on the Day of Resurrection with its color the color of saffron and its scent the scent of musk, bearing the seal of the martyrs. Whoever sincerely asks Allah for martyrdom, Allah gives him the reward of a martyr even if he dies on his bed. Whoever fights in the cause of Allah for the time of milking a camel, Paradise becomes obligatory for him."
"22462","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Mu'adh, he said: Two men reviled one another before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and one of them became angry. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I know a word which, if he said it, his anger would leave: I seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan."
"22463","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi and Abu Said narrated to us, they said: Zaidah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr - and Abu Said said: Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrated to us - from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about a man who met a woman he did not know, and there is nothing a man does with his wife except that he did it with her, except that he did not have intercourse with her? So Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed this verse: Establish prayer at the two ends of the day and in the first hours of the night; indeed good deeds remove evil deeds - the verse. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Perform ablution, then pray. Mu'adh said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, is it for him specifically or for the believers generally? He said: Rather for the believers generally."
"22464","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Qays, from Mu'adh, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever frees a believing neck, it is his ransom from the Fire."
"22465","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Kamil narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Zabyah, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim who sleeps upon the remembrance of Allah, in purity, then wakes in the night and asks Allah for good from the good of this world and the Hereafter, except that He gives it to him."
"22466","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Kamil narrated to us, Hammad, meaning Ibn Salamah, narrated to us, Ata ibn al-Saib informed us, from Abu Razin, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not direct you to a door among the doors of Paradise? I said: Yes. He said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah."
"22467","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, Ibn Jurayj - and Rawh: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa said: Malik ibn Yukhamir narrated to us, that Mu'adh ibn Jabal narrated to him - and Rawh said: He narrated to them - that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever strives in the cause of Allah - and Rawh said: fights in the cause of Allah - among Muslim men, for the time of milking a camel, Paradise has become obligatory for him. Whoever sincerely asks Allah for killing from himself, then dies or is killed, he has the reward of the martyrs. Whoever is wounded with a wound in the cause of Allah, or suffers a calamity, it will come on the Day of Resurrection at its fullest as it was - and Abd al-Razzaq said: like the most intense - and Rawh: like the most abundant - and Hajjaj: like the mightiest as it was - its color like saffron and its scent like musk. Whoever is wounded in the cause of Allah bears the seal of the martyrs."
"22468","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Jabir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Arab villages and commanded me to take the share of the land. Sufyan said: The share of the land is a third and a quarter."
"22469","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Yunus narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to us, from al-Sari ibn Yan'um, from Marih ibn Masruq, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said, when he sent him to Yemen: Beware of luxury, for the servants of Allah are not luxurious."
"22470","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, al-Muqri narrated to us, Haywah narrated to us, he said: I heard Uqbah ibn Muslim al-Tujibi say: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli narrated to me, from al-Sunabihi, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his hand one day, then said: O Mu'adh, I love you. Mu'adh said to him: May my father and mother be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah, and I love you. He said: I enjoin you, O Mu'adh, never to leave saying at the end of every prayer: O Allah, help me to remember You, thank You, and worship You well. And Mu'adh enjoined that upon al-Sunabihi, and al-Sunabihi enjoined it upon Abu Abd al-Rahman, and Abu Abd al-Rahman enjoined it upon Uqbah ibn Muslim."
"22471","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Mis'ar narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from Mu'adh, he said: If Umar was indeed among the people of Paradise - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - whatever he saw in his wakefulness or his sleep is true - and he said: While I was in Paradise, I saw therein a house. I said: Whose is this? It was said: For Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"22472","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Thawban narrated to us, from his father, from Makhul, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Malik ibn Yukhamir, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The flourishing of Bayt al-Maqdis is the ruin of Yathrib, and the ruin of Yathrib is the coming of the great battle, and the coming of the great battle is the conquest of Constantinople, and the conquest of Constantinople is the emergence of the Dajjal. Then he struck with his hand upon the thigh of the one who narrated to him, or his shoulder. Then he said: This is as true as that you are here, or as that you are sitting - meaning Mu'adh."
"22473","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid, meaning Ibn Bahram, narrated to us, Shahr narrated to us, Ibn Ghanm narrated to us, from the hadith of Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with the people toward the expedition of Tabuk. When morning came, he prayed the dawn prayer with the people. Then the people rode. When the sun rose, the people dozed after the night march, while Mu'adh kept close to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), following his tracks, while the people were scattered by their mounts on the sides of the road, grazing and moving. While Mu'adh was following the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his camel grazing at times and moving at others, Mu'adh's camel stumbled, so he restrained it with the rein, and it started up until the camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shied from it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) uncovered his face-covering and turned, and there was no man of the army nearer to him than Mu'adh. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and said: O Mu'adh. He said: At your service, O Prophet of Allah. He said: Come near. So he drew near until their two mounts touched one another. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I did not think the people were as far from us as their place of distance. Mu'adh said: O Prophet of Allah, the people dozed, so their mounts scattered them, grazing and moving. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And I was dozing. When Mu'adh saw the good tidings of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) toward him and his privacy with him, he said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to ask you about a word that has made me ill, sickened me, and grieved me. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ask me about whatever you wish. He said: O Prophet of Allah, narrate to me a deed that will admit me into Paradise; I will not ask you about anything else. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bravo, bravo; you have asked about a great matter - three times - and it is easy for the one for whom Allah intends good, and it is easy for the one for whom Allah intends good, and it is easy for the one for whom Allah intends good. He did not narrate anything to him except that he said it to him three times, meaning he repeated it to him three times out of care that he learn it well from him. So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You believe in Allah and the Last Day, and establish prayer, and worship Allah alone, not associating anything with Him, until you die upon that. He said: O Prophet of Allah, repeat for me. So he repeated it to him three times. Then the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you wish, I will narrate to you, O Mu'adh, the head of this matter, and the support of this matter, and the peak of the hump. Mu'adh said: Yes, may my father and mother be ransom for you, O Prophet of Allah, so narrate to me. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The head of this matter is that you testify that there is no god but Allah alone, with no partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. The support of this matter is establishing prayer and giving zakat. The peak of its hump is jihad in the cause of Allah. I was commanded to fight the people until they establish prayer, and give zakat, and testify that there is no god but Allah alone, with no partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. When they do that, they have protected and safeguarded their blood and their wealth except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah, mighty and majestic. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, no face has paled, nor foot become dusty, in a deed seeking thereby the ranks of Paradise after the obligatory prayer, like jihad in the cause of Allah. Nor is the scale of a servant weighed down like an animal that dies for him in the cause of Allah, or that he carries upon in the cause of Allah."
"22474","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Yunus narrated to us, Fulayh narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Mu'adh, that the prayer passed through three stages. Then he mentioned its stages, only."
"22475","Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, and Yazid ibn Harun: al-Mas'udi informed us - Abu al-Nadr said in his hadith: Amr ibn Murrah narrated to me - from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: Prayer passed through three stages, and fasting passed through three stages. As for the stages of prayer: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah while praying seventeen months toward Bayt al-Maqdis. Then Allah revealed to him: We see the turning of your face toward the heaven, so We will surely turn you to a qiblah that pleases you; so turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque, and wherever you are, turn your faces toward it. So Allah directed him to Makkah. This is one stage. They used to gather for prayer, with some calling others to it, until they struck the clapper, or almost struck it. Then a man of the Ansar called Abdullah ibn Zayd came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw in what the sleeper sees - and had I said I was not asleep I would have spoken truly - while I was between sleep and wakefulness, I saw a person wearing two green garments. He faced the qiblah and said: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; I testify there is no god but Allah, I testify there is no god but Allah, twice twice, until he finished the adhan. Then he waited a while. Then he said the like of what he said, except that he adds therein: The prayer has begun, the prayer has begun. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Teach it to Bilal, and let him call with it. So Bilal was the first to call with it. Umar ibn al-Khattab came and said: O Messenger of Allah, the like of what surrounded him has surrounded me, except that he preceded me. So these are two stages. They used to come to prayer while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had preceded them in part of it. A man would gesture to a man when he came: How much did he pray? He would say: One, or two. So he would pray it, then enter with the people in their prayer. Then Mu'adh came and said: I never find him in any state except that I am in it, then I make up what preceded me. Then he came while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had preceded him in part of it. So he stood firm with him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he stood and made up. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Mu'adh has instituted a practice for you; do so. So these are three stages. As for the stages of fasting: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah and began fasting three days of every month - and Yazid said: He fasted seventeen months, from Rabi' al-Awwal to Ramadan, three days of every month, and fasted the day of Ashura. Then Allah, mighty and majestic, made fasting obligatory upon him. Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: O you who believe, fasting is prescribed for you as it was prescribed for those before you - to this verse: And upon those who can bear it, a ransom of feeding a poor person. So whoever wished fasted, and whoever wished fed a poor person, and that sufficed for him. Then Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed the other verse: The month of Ramadan in which the Quran was revealed - to His saying: So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it. So Allah affirmed its fasting upon the resident who is healthy, and permitted it for the sick and the traveler, and affirmed feeding for the elderly who cannot fast. So these are two stages. They used to eat, drink, and approach women so long as they had not slept; when they slept, they abstained. Then a man of the Ansar called Sirmah worked fasting until evening. He came to his family, prayed Isha, then slept, and did not eat nor drink until morning, and awoke fasting. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him exhausted with severe exhaustion and said: Why do I see you exhausted with severe exhaustion? He said: O Messenger of Allah, I worked yesterday, and when I came I threw myself down and slept, and awoke fasting. And Umar had taken from women, from a slave girl or a free woman, after sleeping, and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. So Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: Permitted to you on the night of fasting is intercourse with your wives - to His saying: Then complete the fast until night. And Yazid said: He fasted nineteen months, from Rabi' al-Awwal to Ramadan."
"22476","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, from Zaidah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed a prayer and perfected the standing, humility, bowing, and prostration in it. He said: It is a prayer of desire and fear. I asked Allah in it for three things: He gave me two and withheld one from me. I asked Him not to send against my nation an enemy outside of them who would destroy them, and He gave me it. I asked Him not to send upon them a year that kills them with hunger, and He gave me it. I asked Him not to make their violence among themselves, and He returned it to me."
"22477","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Asim narrated to us, Haywah narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Muslim narrated to me, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli narrated to us, from al-Sunabihi, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me and said: O Mu'adh, I love you. I said: O Messenger of Allah, and I, by Allah, love you. He said: I enjoin upon you words to say in every prayer: O Allah, help me to remember You, thank You, and worship You well."
"22478","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Asim narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Salih ibn Abi Arib, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from Mu'adh, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has as his last speech: There is no god but Allah, Paradise becomes obligatory for him."
"22479","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Amir al-Aslami narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Mu'adh, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek refuge in Allah from greed that leads to disposition, and from greed in what is not a place of greed, and from greed where there is no place of greed."
"22480","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Wail, from Mu'adh, that he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen to take from every thirty cattle a cow, a tabi' or tabi'ah - or he said: a jadha' or jadh'ah - and from every forty cattle a cow, a musinnah, and from every adult a dinar or its equivalent in ma'afir garments."
"22481","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Yahya ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, Rishdin narrated to us, from Zabban, from Sahl, from his father, from Mu'adh, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the best of faith. He said: That you love for Allah, and hate for Allah, and employ your tongue in the remembrance of Allah. He said: What else, O Messenger of Allah? He said: That you love for people what you love for yourself, and hate for them what you hate for yourself."
"22482","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Mu'adh, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), transmitting from Allah, mighty and majestic, He said: My love is due for those who love one another in Me, and sit together in Me, and give to one another in Me."
"22483","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hasan narrated to us, Ibn Luhay'ah narrated to us, Zabban ibn Faid narrated to us, from Sahl ibn Mu'adh, from his father, that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the best of faith. He said: The best of faith is that you love for Allah, and hate for Allah, and employ your tongue in the remembrance of Allah. He said: What else, O Messenger of Allah? He said: That you love for people what you love for yourself, and hate for them what you hate for yourself, and that you speak good or remain silent."
"22484","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Surayj narrated to us, Hammad, meaning Ibn Salamah, narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Mu'adh, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I will inform you of doors of good: Fasting is a shield, and charity extinguishes sin as water extinguishes fire, and the servant standing in prayer at night. Then he recited: Their sides forsake their beds - to the end of the verse."
"22485","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Surayj narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, from Ammar ibn Muhammad al-Absi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Mu'adh, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on one of his journeys, he heard a caller saying: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest. So he said: Upon the natural disposition. He said: I testify there is no god but Allah. So he said: He has testified the testimony of truth. He said: I testify that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. He said: He has gone out of the Fire. Look; you will find him either a shepherd staying in the open, or a keeper of dogs. So they looked and found him a shepherd; prayer had come upon him, so he called for it."
"22486","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Kamil narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Tawus, from Mu'adh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not say anything concerning the awqas of cattle."
"22487","Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ismail narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, that the plague fell in Sham. Amr ibn al-As said: This impurity has fallen, so flee from it into the mountain paths and valleys. That reached Mu'adh, and he did not believe him in what he said. So he said: Rather it is martyrdom, and mercy, and the supplication of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). O Allah, give Mu'adh and his family their share of Your mercy. Abu Qilabah said: I understood martyrdom and understood mercy, but I did not know what the supplication of your Prophet is, until I was informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while one night praying, said in his supplication: Then fever, then, or plague; then fever, then, or plague - three times. When morning came, a man of his family said to him: O Messenger of Allah, I heard you last night supplicating with a supplication. He said: And you heard it? He said: Yes. He said: I asked my Lord, mighty and majestic, not to destroy my nation with a year of famine, and He gave me it. I asked Him not to empower over them an enemy outside of them who would violate them, and He gave me it. I asked Him not to clothe them in factions and make them taste the violence of one another, and He refused me - or he said: He withheld it from me. So I said: Then fever, then, or plague; then fever, then, or plague; then fever, then, or plague - three times."
